    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Шеф, я понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Boss, I got it.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'chef, boss' paired with the pronoun for 'I'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вы добавили базилик, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You added basil, right?' Fedya turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'to add', followed by 'basil'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Чист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Clear.' the inspector delivers a punchy conversational reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'clean'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Проверили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Checked.' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я так и знал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I knew it.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'knew'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, ошибочка вышла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, a mistake happened.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Viktor' paired with the proper name for 'Petrovich'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мои люди проверили этого человека.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My people checked this person.' The phrasing opens with 'people' before bringing in 'checked' and 'a man' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Абсолютно чист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Completely clean.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'completely' directly with 'clean', combining 'completely' with 'clean'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А я всегда знал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And I always knew.' Chef Barinov puts the word for 'always' together with 'knew' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Федя, ты как?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fedya, how are you?' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Fedya' paired with the pronoun for 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Нормально.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine.' Fedya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'fine'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ты как меня вытащил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How did you get me out?' Fedya expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'how' with 'to drag out'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ключи только у шефа были.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Only the boss had keys.' Fedya puts the word for 'the keys' together with 'only' and 'chef' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пусть он так думает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let him think so.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'thinks'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
